  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="what-title" data-split>What is an AI-enhanced UX/UI audit?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>An AI-enhanced UX/UI audit is a senior designer's review of a live product, where AI tools do the legwork of organising, cross-referencing and packaging the findings, and the designer makes every judgement. The result is a prioritised list of problems and recommendations, each tied to a screen, graded by severity and effort, and sequenced into what to fix first.</p>
          <p>Echopod is how we show one. It is a real client audit, anonymised end to end: same structure, same method, invented names and numbers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="problem-title" data-split>The problem</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>The app had a strong idea and a working product, but it had grown screen by screen. There was no brand foundation, so it read as generic AI. Each tab solved its own problems in its own way, and the website and App Store page told a different story again.</p>
          <p>The team needed more than a list of complaints. They needed to know which problems mattered, how hard each one was to fix, and where to start.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="did-title" data-split>How did we run the audit?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Map first, judge second.</strong> We mapped the product before writing a single finding: seven in-app zones, the design system layer across them, and the website and App Store page. Then 44 user flows in four tiers, from the core create, listen and share loop down to system flows like errors and consent.</li>
            <li><strong>Several sources, one review.</strong> Every finding was checked against the live Figma build, the support inbox and a set of competitor apps.</li>
            <li><strong>One format per finding.</strong> Context, problem, recommendation, evidence and a link to the Figma node, so each one is a decision path, not an opinion.</li>
            <li><strong>Graded and plotted.</strong> Each item has a severity (critical, high, normal, low) and an effort estimate, which places it on a value and effort matrix: quick wins, sprint scope, polish, or skip.</li>
            <li><strong>Caveats before findings.</strong> The report says up front what it doesn't claim: recommendations are directional, not build-ready specs, legal questions belong to the client's lawyers, and it is a snapshot in time.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

